flat:
And flung me on my hill flat on my face;
Flat on my face I lay defying pain,
Glad of the blood in my smallest vein,

flags:
The fields are snowbound no longer;
There are little blue lakes and flags of tenderest green.
The snow has been caught up into the sky--

They drew the gurgling water from its deep;
Lizzie plucked purple and rich golden flags,
Then turning homeward said: 'The sunset flushes

flank:
Smooth endless days, notched here and there with knives;
A weary, wormy darkness, spurred i’ the flank
With flame, that it should eat and end itself

flashes:
There is internal evidence that many of the poems were simply
spontaneous flashes of insight, apparently unrelated to outward
circumstance. Others, however, had an obvious personal origin;

flake:
Of day the little gray feet know no rest;
Through the lone night until the last snow-flake
Has dropped from heaven upon the earth’s white breast,

flakes:
He munches his store in the old hollow tree;
Tho' cold is the blast and the snow-flakes are drifty
He fears the white flock not a whit more than we.

flashed:
She told, in secret and with whispering,
How he had flashed a mirror in her eyes;
And as she told, it struck her with surprise

flag:
And bathed them in the glee;
The East put out a single flag,
And signed the fete away.

flattens:
All that I really love
Is the rain that flattens on the bay,
And the eel-grass in the cove;

flawless:
And all your beauty stand you in no stead;
This flawless, vital hand, this perfect head,
This body of flame and steel, before the gust

flaming:
With such a dawn of conscience. For the heart,
Made firewood for his sake, and flaming up
To his very face ... he warmed his feet at it;

flattest:
The pillar box is high;
It has the flattest sort of head
And not a nose or eye,

flare:
I see the oak woods at Saxton's flaming
In a flare of crimson by the frost newly fired;
And I am thirsty for the spring in the valley

flail:
I will not rant, I will not rail;
For good the grain must feel the flail;
There are whom love has never blessed.

I am smiling one minute and sighing the next;
And if it goes on, I 'll drop hackle and flail,
And go to the parson and tell him my tale.

flash'd:
They brought her message to the King;
Dark flash'd his angry eye;
'Twas as the lightning ere the storm

flambeaux:
Till the houses blot;
And the odd flambeaux no men carry
Glimmer on the spot!

flagons:
Bring me the sunset in a cup,
Reckon the morning's flagons up,
And say how many dew;

A coronet to find;
Who never thirsted, flagons
And cooling tamarind.
